  'lead' => 'A virtual assistant can make Google Ads for small business easier to manage by handling campaign setup, keyword research, ad checks, reporting and routine optimisation. The key is having one person responsible for the process instead of leaving your advertising scattered across tools and tasks.',
  'body' => '<p>For a small business owner, Google Ads can be useful when you need people to find your business for a specific product or service. But running campaigns involves more than writing an ad: you need to choose the right searches, set budgets, monitor results, improve landing pages and act on the data.</p><p>That is where a virtual assistant can help. Instead of asking you to become an expert in every part of Google Ads, a dedicated person can handle the routine work, keep the campaign organised and give you a clear view of what is happening.</p><h2 id="what-does-a-virtual-assistant-do-for-google-ads">What does a virtual assistant do for Google Ads?</h2><p>A virtual assistant supporting Google Ads can take care of the operational work around your campaigns. Depending on the arrangement, this can include research, campaign setup, monitoring, reporting and coordination with other marketing or development work.</p><p>The role is not simply to "run ads." A useful VA should understand what your business is trying to achieve and connect advertising activity to measurable outcomes such as enquiries, calls, purchases or qualified leads.</p><h3 id="typical-tasks-a-va-can-handle">Typical tasks a VA can handle</h3><ul><li>Researching keywords and search intent</li><li>Organising campaigns and ad groups</li><li>Writing and testing ad variations</li><li>Checking budgets and campaign settings</li><li>Reviewing search terms and adding negative keywords</li><li>Monitoring clicks, conversions and cost per result</li><li>Preparing weekly or monthly performance reports</li><li>Coordinating landing-page, tracking or design changes with specialists</li></ul><aside class="blg-post-callout"><p class="blg-post-callout-label">Key takeaway</p><p>A good virtual assistant reduces the amount of routine Google Ads work on your plate while keeping you informed about where your advertising budget is going.</p></aside><h2 id="why-google-ads-for-small-business-needs-regular-attention">Why Google Ads for small business needs regular attention</h2><p>Google Ads is not a set-it-and-forget-it activity. A campaign that looks fine when launched can collect irrelevant searches, spend unevenly across keywords or send visitors to a page that does not make the next step clear.</p><p>Small businesses also have less room for wasted spending. If your monthly advertising budget is limited, checking campaigns regularly can help you identify obvious problems before they continue for weeks.</p><h3 id="start-with-one-clear-business-goal">Start with one clear business goal</h3><p>Before creating a campaign, decide what you want the advertising to produce. A local service business might want phone calls or enquiry forms, while an online store may care about completed purchases.</p><p>Do not measure every campaign with the same metric. Ten thousand impressions are not automatically useful if the campaign is supposed to generate enquiries. Your tracking should reflect the actual business action you care about.</p><h3 id="separate-useful-searches-from-irrelevant-ones">Separate useful searches from irrelevant ones</h3><p>Keyword research is only the starting point. After a campaign receives traffic, review the actual search terms that triggered your ads. Some may be highly relevant, while others may have a different intent from what you offer.</p><p>Adding suitable negative keywords can help reduce irrelevant traffic. This is one of the routine tasks a VA can monitor as new search data becomes available.</p><h2 id="how-to-use-a-virtual-assistant-to-manage-your-google-ads-wor">How to use a virtual assistant to manage your Google Ads workflow</h2><p>The easiest way to make the arrangement work is to give the VA a defined process rather than simply saying, "Please manage my ads." You should agree on the target audience, services being advertised, budget range, conversion goals and reporting schedule.</p><h3 id="step-1-document-the-business-and-offer">Step 1: Document the business and offer</h3><p>Give the VA basic information about your customers, locations, products or services, pricing, competitive advantages and the action you want visitors to take. This information helps prevent generic ad copy and irrelevant keyword choices.</p><h3 id="step-2-set-up-tracking-before-judging-performance">Step 2: Set up tracking before judging performance</h3><p>Make sure important actions are measurable. Depending on your business, that might include form submissions, phone calls, purchases or another conversion event.</p><p>If you cannot connect ad clicks with meaningful actions, it becomes difficult to decide whether the campaign is helping the business. A VA can coordinate with a developer when website or tracking changes are needed.</p><h3 id="step-3-build-a-focused-campaign">Step 3: Build a focused campaign</h3><p>Start with a manageable set of products, services or locations instead of trying to advertise everything at once. Focused campaigns make it easier to understand which searches and messages are producing useful traffic.</p><p>For example, a Kota-based home-service business could separate campaigns for different high-intent services rather than putting every service into one broad campaign.</p><h3 id="step-4-review-performance-on-a-schedule">Step 4: Review performance on a schedule</h3><p>The review frequency should depend on the budget, traffic volume and campaign activity. A small campaign may need a few meaningful checks each week rather than constant changes throughout the day.</p><p>Look at spending, search terms, conversions, cost per conversion, ad relevance and landing-page behaviour. Avoid changing multiple major settings at once simply because performance moved for a few days.</p><h3 id="step-5-turn-reports-into-decisions">Step 5: Turn reports into decisions</h3><p>A report should tell you what happened and what should happen next. Instead of sending a spreadsheet full of numbers, your VA can highlight changes such as which campaigns spent the most, which produced conversions and which areas need investigation.</p><h2 id="google-ads-management-vs-doing-everything-yourself">Google Ads management vs doing everything yourself</h2><p>Hiring a virtual assistant does not mean giving up control of your advertising account. You can retain ownership of the account, approve budgets and receive regular reports while delegating repetitive work.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>What you handle</th><th>What to consider</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Do it yourself</td><td>     Research, setup, monitoring,  <br>       and reporting</td><td>Lower direct service cost, but more time and learning required</td></tr><tr><td>General VA support</td><td>Routine research, reporting and campaign administration</td><td>Works best when the VA has clear processes and appropriate Google Ads knowledge</td></tr><tr><td>Specialist-managed campaigns</td><td>Strategy and technical campaign decisions handled by specialists</td><td>Higher service cost may be appropriate for more complex accounts</td></tr></tbody></table><p>There is no universal budget or staffing model. If your account is small and simple, you may only need limited support. If you have multiple locations, products, campaigns or conversion paths, the workload can grow quickly.</p><h2 id="how-much-does-google-ads-support-cost-for-a-small-business">How much does Google Ads support cost for a small business?</h2><p>The cost depends on the amount of work, campaign complexity, advertising budget and whether you need only administration or broader marketing support. In India, small-business support arrangements can range from a few thousand rupees per month for limited assistance to substantially more for ongoing specialist management.</p><p>Your Google Ads media budget is separate from the fee you pay for management or VA support. For example, if you allocate ₹20,000 per month to advertising, that does not automatically include the cost of the person managing the campaigns.</p><p>Before agreeing to a monthly arrangement, ask exactly what is included. A simple scope might cover campaign monitoring, search-term reviews and monthly reporting, while a larger scope could include landing pages, conversion tracking, ad creative, SEO and wider marketing coordination.</p><h2 id="common-google-ads-mistakes-small-businesses-make">Common Google Ads mistakes small businesses make</h2><p>Many problems come from process rather than the advertising platform itself. A small business can have a reasonable campaign structure but still waste money if the offer, targeting, tracking or landing page is not aligned.</p><ul><li><strong>Starting without conversion tracking:</strong> You may know that people clicked but not whether they contacted or bought from you.</li><li><strong>Using overly broad keywords:</strong> Broad targeting can bring searches that do not match your actual service.</li><li><strong>Sending every visitor to the homepage:</strong> A dedicated landing page can make the next action clearer when the search is highly specific.</li><li><strong>Ignoring search terms:</strong> Actual search queries can reveal irrelevant traffic and new opportunities.</li><li><strong>Changing campaigns too frequently:</strong> Constant changes make it harder to understand which adjustment affected performance.</li><li><strong>Judging results from clicks alone:</strong> Traffic matters, but the business outcome should remain the main focus.</li><li><strong>Giving access without a process:</strong> Someone having account access does not mean there is a clear plan for managing the account.</li></ul><aside class="blg-post-callout"><p class="blg-post-callout-label">Key takeaway</p><p>The best VA workflow combines regular campaign checks with clear business goals, reliable conversion tracking and simple reporting that helps you make decisions.</p></aside><h2 id="connect-google-ads-with-the-rest-of-your-marketing">Connect Google Ads with the rest of your marketing</h2><p>Paid advertising works better as part of a wider customer journey. Someone may click an ad today, compare your business with competitors, return through organic search later and finally contact you through another channel.</p><p>That is why you may also need <a href="https://web-sandbox.oaiusercontent.com/services/online-marketing/search-engine-optimization" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">search engine optimisation services</a> to build long-term organic visibility, or <a href="https://web-sandbox.oaiusercontent.com/services/online-marketing/content-marketing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">content marketing support</a> for useful pages that answer customer questions.</p><p>If your website is slow, confusing or difficult to use, improving the advertising campaign alone may not solve the underlying problem. In that situation, <a href="https://web-sandbox.oaiusercontent.com/services/development/website-development" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">professional website development</a> can address technical and conversion issues on the site itself.</p><p>You can also use <a href="https://web-sandbox.oaiusercontent.com/services/online-marketing/analytics-and-reporting" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">analytics and reporting services</a> to bring marketing data together and understand how different channels contribute to your goals.</p><p>For businesses that want someone to coordinate these moving parts, ScaleSphere uses a dedicated Virtual Assistant as the main point of contact, backed by marketing, development and design specialists. The VA can keep communication and routine tasks organised while specialists handle work that requires deeper expertise.</p><h2 id="a-simple-checklist-before-hiring-google-ads-support">A simple checklist before hiring Google Ads support</h2><p>Before you give someone responsibility for your campaigns, check whether you have the basics in place:</p><ol><li>Define the business goal and primary conversion.</li><li>Set a monthly advertising budget that you are comfortable testing.</li><li>Confirm who owns the Google Ads account and billing.</li><li>Make sure conversion tracking is configured and tested.</li><li>List the products, services and locations you actually want to promote.</li><li>Agree on what the VA will manage and what requires specialist approval.</li><li>Choose a reporting schedule and decide which metrics matter.</li><li>Review the arrangement after enough campaign data has accumulated to make useful decisions.</li></ol><p>The right setup is less about finding someone who performs every task alone and more about creating clear ownership. You should know who monitors the account, who makes strategic decisions, who handles website changes and how performance is reported.</p><p>For a small business, that structure can make Google Ads easier to operate without requiring the owner to spend every week inside the advertising platform.</p>',
